## The Four Borrow Patterns

### Pattern 1: Read-Only Inspection
Use `&` when the function only reads data:
```xiom
fn inspect(item: &Inventory) -> Int
  ensures: result >= 0;
{
  return item.count;
}
```
Safe, concurrent, zero-cost. The most common pattern.

### Pattern 2: Mutation
Use `&mut` when the function changes data:
```xiom
fn restock(item: &mut Inventory, qty: Int)
  requires: qty > 0;
  ensures: item.count == item@pre.count + qty;
{
  item.count = item.count + qty;
}
```
Only one `&mut` exists at a time. The compiler enforces this.

### Pattern 3: Iteration
Borrow the collection immutably, then iterate:
```xiom
fn sum_all(items: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  let total = 0;
  for item in items {
    total = total + item;
  }
  return total;
}
```
No mutation while iterating — the compiler won't let you.

### Pattern 4: Chaining
Pass a mutable borrow through a chain of functions:
```xiom
fn process_order(inv: &mut Inventory, orders: &Vec[Int])
  requires: orders.len() > 0;
{
  for qty in orders {
    sell(inv, qty);
  }
}
```
`sell` takes `&mut`, so `process_order` must also take `&mut`. The borrow follows the call chain.

### Pattern Matching in Practice
Recognize the pattern → apply the correct borrow → let contracts handle the logic. The compiler handles the rest.

